{"name":"Cinder","hex":"#242a2e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Cinder","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A river in Alaska.","A female given name."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Cinder"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ember","blocks":[{"text":"An ember, also called a hot coal, is a hot lump of smouldering solid fuel, typically glowing, composed of greatly heated wood, coal, or other carbon-based material. Embers can exist within, remain after, or sometimes precede, a fire. Embers are, in some cases, as hot as the fire which created them. They radiate a substantial amount of heat long after the fire has been extinguished, and if not taken care of properly can rekindle a fire that is thought to be completely extinguished and can pose a fire hazard. In order to avoid the danger of accidentally spreading a fire, many campers pour water on the embers or cover them in dirt. Alternatively, embers can be used to relight a fire after it has gone out without the need to rebuild the fire – in a conventional fireplace, a fire can easily be relit up to 12 hours after it goes out, provided that there is enough space for air to circulate between the embers and the introduced fuel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Embers_01.JPG/330px-Embers_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ember"},{"title":"Ash","blocks":[{"text":"Ash is the solid remnants of fires. Specifically, ash refers to all non-aqueous, non-gaseous residues that remain after something burns. In analytical chemistry, to analyse the mineral and metal content of chemical samples, ash is the non-gaseous, non-liquid residue after complete combustion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Wood_ash.jpg/330px-Wood_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ash"},{"title":"Cinder (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Cinder is the 2012 debut young adult science fiction novel of American author Marissa Meyer, published by Macmillan Publishers through their subsidiary Feiwel & Friends. It is the first book in The Lunar Chronicles and is followed by Scarlet. The story is loosely based on the classic fairytale Cinderella. Cinder was selected as one of IndieBound's Kids' Next List for winter 2012."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1b/Cinder_%28Official_Book_Cover%29_by_Marissa_Meyer.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinder_(novel)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Bonfire_Embers.jpg/500px-Bonfire_Embers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Bonfire_Embers.jpg/500px-Bonfire_Embers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A bonfire in rural Australia, with a large number of sparks being blown by the wind; sparks are akin to embers in a similar way that dust is to stone.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABonfire_Embers.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg/500px-Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg/1280px-Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg/500px-Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Embers of firewood used in sauna stove","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/com